          "completed": 1790854292818
        },
        "finish": "stop",
        "id": "msg_0f73bb0e0001BzHNNlm2HATu3P",
        "sessionID": "ses_f08c46ba3ffeXIM05O83TMKJzd"
      },
      "parts": [
        {
          "type": "step-start",
          "id": "prt_0f73bc295001Htzvf1sDmjmk7R",
          "sessionID": "ses_f08c46ba3ffeXIM05O83TMKJzd",
          "messageID": "msg_0f73bb0e0001BzHNNlm2HATu3P"
        },
        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The current implementation:\n\n```python\ndef overlaps(left, right):\n    \"\"\"Return whether two intervals share an integer point.\"\"\"\n    return max(left[0], right[0]) <= min(left[1], right[1])\n```\n\nFor half-open intervals [start, end), two intervals overlap iff max(starts) < min(ends). The current code uses `<=`, which incorrectly reports touching intervals like [0,1) and [1,2) as overlapping. Also, empty intervals: an empty interval [2,2) — max(left[0], right[0]) <= min(left[1], right[1])? With [2,2) vs [5,6): max(2,5)=5 <= min(2,6)=2 → False, good. But [2,2) vs [1,3): max(2,1)=2 <= min(2,3)=2 → True, but [2,2) is empty and shares no integer point, so must be False. Fix: use strict `<`.\n\nSo replace `<=` with `<`. That fixes both touching and empty intervals:\n- [0,1) vs [1,2): max=1, min=1 → 1 < 1 False. Good.\n- [2,2) vs [1,3): max=2, min=2 → 2 < 2 False. Good.\n- [0,2) vs [1,3): max=1, min=2 → 1 < 2 True. Good.\n- Disjoint [0,1) vs [2,3): max=2, min=1 → False. Good.\n\nThe docstring \"share an integer point\" — empty intervals share no integer point, and touching half-open intervals share no integer point. So strict `<` matches the documented semantics.\n\nLet me make the replacement.",
          "time": {
            "start": 1790854283927,
            "end": 1790854291664
          },
          "id": "prt_0f73bc297001ziUd6WhYn7tHNh",
          "sessionID": "ses_f08c46ba3ffeXIM05O83TMKJzd",
          "messageID": "msg_0f73bb0e0001BzHNNlm2HATu3P"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The bug is clear: `overlaps` uses `<=`, which treats touching intervals (e.g., [0,1) and [1,2)) and empty intervals (e.g., [2,2) vs [1,3)) as overlapping. For half-open intervals sharing an integer point, the condition must be strict: `max(starts) < min(ends)`. I'll make that minimal edit, preserving the API and `span`. {\"action\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"intervals.py\",\"old\":\"    return max(left[0], right[0]) <= min(left[1], right[1])\",\"new\":\"    return max(left[0], right[0]) < min(left[1], right[1])\"}",
          "time": {
            "start": 1790854291665,
            "end": 1790854292812
          },
          "id": "prt_0f73be0d10016Eba0R5PEN8b2M",
          "sessionID": "ses_f08c46ba3ffeXIM05O83TMKJzd",
          "messageID": "msg_0f73bb0e0001BzHNNlm2HATu3P"
        },
        {
